# Jones Framework



%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
# Jones Framework



%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## Derivation of the calibrated sample Jones matrix

For an ideal linear polarizer at angle $\\theta$, define the Jones vector

$$
\\mathbf e(\\theta)=
\\begin{pmatrix}
\\cos\\theta \\\\
\\sin\\theta
\\end{pmatrix}.
$$

For a measured $2\\times2$ transfer matrix

$$
\\mathbf T=
\\begin{pmatrix}
t_{00} & t_{01} \\\\
t_{10} & t_{11}
\\end{pmatrix},
$$

the complex field measured with the analyzer at $\\alpha$ and the input polarizer at $\\beta$ is

$$
m_{\\alpha,\\beta}
=
\\mathbf e^{\\mathsf T}(\\alpha)\\,
\\mathbf T\\,
\\mathbf e(\\beta).
$$

The four measured configurations yield

$$
\\begin{aligned}
M_0
&=m_{0^\\circ,0^\\circ}=t_{00},\\\\
M_{90}
&=m_{90^\\circ,90^\\circ}=t_{11},\\\\
M_{45,0}
&=m_{45^\\circ,0^\\circ}
=\\frac{t_{00}+t_{10}}{\\sqrt 2},\\\\
M_{45,90}
&=m_{45^\\circ,90^\\circ}
=\\frac{t_{01}+t_{11}}{\\sqrt 2}.
\\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the two unmeasured matrix elements are

$$
t_{10}=\\sqrt 2\\,M_{45,0}-M_0,
\\qquad
t_{01}=\\sqrt 2\\,M_{45,90}-M_{90}.
$$

The complete transfer matrix measured with the sample installed is therefore

$$
\\mathbf T_{\\mathrm{sam}}=
\\begin{pmatrix}
M_0 &
\\sqrt 2\\,M_{45,90}-M_{90}\\\\
\\sqrt 2\\,M_{45,0}-M_0 &
M_{90}
\\end{pmatrix}.
$$

The reference measurements give the analogous matrix

$$
\\mathbf T_{\\mathrm{ref}}=
\\begin{pmatrix}
R_0 &
\\sqrt 2\\,R_{45,90}-R_{90}\\\\
\\sqrt 2\\,R_{45,0}-R_0 &
R_{90}
\\end{pmatrix}.
$$

In the convention used here, the reference (system) response acts on the left of the sample Jones matrix:

$$
\\mathbf T_{\\mathrm{sam}}
=
\\mathbf T_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\,
\\mathbf J_{\\mathrm{sam}}.
$$

Provided that

$$
\\det\\!\\left(\\mathbf T_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right)\\neq0,
$$

solving the matrix equation from the left yields

$$
\\boxed{
\\mathbf J_{\\mathrm{sam}}
=
\\mathbf T_{\\mathrm{ref}}^{-1}
\\mathbf T_{\\mathrm{sam}}
}.
$$

Numerically, the notebook evaluates this as

$$
\\boxed{
\\texttt{LinearSolve[Treference, Tsample]}
}
$$

rather than explicitly forming the inverse. This is both clearer and numerically more stable. The order is essential because Jones matrices generally do not commute:

$$
\\mathbf T_{\\mathrm{ref}}^{-1}\\mathbf T_{\\mathrm{sam}}
\\neq
\\mathbf T_{\\mathrm{sam}}\\mathbf T_{\\mathrm{ref}}^{-1}.
$$


