.md
### Extracting Material Parameters
Assuming that the ellipticity is caused by optical misalignment, we can neglect it and use linearized eigenvectors:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### Extracting Material Parameters
Assuming that the ellipticity is caused by optical misalignment, we can neglect it and use linearized eigenvectors:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
basis = OpticalAxisAnalysis[d, Quantity[Interval[{1.15, 1.5}], "Terahertz"]]["LinearizedEigenvectors"]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
{{0.9997855134235536`,0.020710556448375564`},{-0.040682908928530645`,0.9991721077577741`}}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
When expressed in `basis`, our $2\\times 2$ transmission matrix becomes nearly diagonal. Repeating this process at every frequency allows us to extract the sample transmission for each optical eigenmode:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
When expressed in `basis`, our $2\\times 2$ transmission matrix becomes nearly diagonal. Repeating this process at every frequency allows us to extract the sample transmission for each optical eigenmode:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
t1122 = OpticalAxesTransmission[d, basis];
FrequencySpectrumTrim /@ %;
ListLinePlot[%[[All, All, {1, 2}]],
  PlotRange -> {{0.25, 1.55}, {0, 1}},
  Frame -> True,
  FrameLabel -> {"\[Omega] (THz)", "t(\[Omega])"},
  PlotLegends -> {"Polarization 1", "Polarization 2"}
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(Legended[ToExpression[FrontEndRef["Fbf1786b5-d46a-4e36-9fd8-c722891df115"], InputForm], Placed[LineLegend[{Directive[PointSize[0.006944444444444445], RGBColor[0.24, 0.6, 0.8], AbsoluteThickness[2]], Directive[PointSize[0.006944444444444445], RGBColor[0.95, 0.627, 0.1425], AbsoluteThickness[2]]}, {"Pol 1", "Pol 2"}, LegendMarkers -> {{False, Automatic}, {False, Automatic}}, Joined -> {True, True}, LabelStyle -> {}, LegendLayout -> "Column"], After, Identity]])(*,*)(*"1:"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Here is what these two components look like in the time domain:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Here is what these two components look like in the time domain:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
BandPassFilter[Quantity[0.7, "Terahertz"], 2.5] /@ FD2TD /@ t1122;
ListLinePlot[%[[All, ;; 600]], PlotRange->Full, 
  Frame->True, FrameLabel->{"\[Tau] (ps)", "t(\[Tau])"},
  PlotLegends->{"Pol 1", "Pol 2"}]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
